# Error Handling & Validation

- Never swallow errors. Empty `catch` blocks are forbidden — handle, log with context, or rethrow with context.
- Fail fast: validate at system boundaries (API handlers, forms, external data) with schema validation (Zod), not ad-hoc checks.
- Log messages must be actionable: what failed, why, and identifiers. Never log generic "Something went wrong".
- Distinguish expected errors (validation, not-found) from unexpected ones (bugs, outages). Typed results or specific error classes for expected cases; unexpected errors go to a central handler.
- Every async operation has explicit error handling. No floating promises.
- External calls (HTTP, DB, file I/O) always have timeouts and, where appropriate, retries with backoff.
- Never expose internals (stack traces, SQL, file paths) in user-facing errors or API responses.
- Clean up resources in `finally` or language idioms so failures do not leak handles.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
try {
  await fetchVehicle(id);
} catch {}

// ✅ GOOD
try {
  const parsed = VehicleIdSchema.parse(id);
  return await vehicleRepository.getById(parsed, { timeoutMs: 5_000 });
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof VehicleNotFoundError) return { ok: false, code: "not_found" };
  logger.error(`Failed to load vehicle ${id}: ${errorMessage(error)}`);
  throw new VehicleLoadError(`Failed to load vehicle ${id}`, { cause: error });
}
```
